  The balance CANNOT close on an ISOTHERMAL solve: T is an INPUT, so the isothermal
  constraint is itself an unmodelled heat sink, and P17.4 supplies no stress tensor
  for the viscous-work term. 'predicted' is the INDEPENDENTLY computed mass-weighted
  kinetic-energy gain from inlet to outlet; its agreement with 'E imbalance' is the
  real check, and turns an unclosable balance into a measurement.

### G5's ACTUAL energy criterion: the KINETIC contribution, relative to itself ###
U0      ke_comp [J]     ke_inc [J]      relDiff        noise floor    signal/noise
1       5.4589667       5.4580807       0.00016233     0.0053567      46.97       
0.5     1.3670556       1.3669489       7.8081e-05     0.0024803      12.1        
0.25    0.34205592      0.34204276      3.8465e-05     0.0011395      3.182       
0.125   0.085550747     0.085549087     1.9409e-05     0.00054146     0.8199      
  relDiff is the domain kinetic energy of the COMPRESSIBLE solution against the
  INCOMPRESSIBLE one, normalised by the latter -- the kinetic contribution measured
  against its OWN magnitude, exactly as the frozen G5 clause requires.
  'noise floor' is |massImbalance| * h, the enthalpy-flux error the residual mass
  imbalance implies. Where signal/noise falls below ~1 the BALANCE cannot reveal the
  kinetic term at all -- which is why the criterion is placed on ke directly.

